<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What the signed agent is allowed to do, under the hardened runtime.

  DELIBERATELY ONE ENTITLEMENT. Every key here trades away a protection, and
  the usual advice — paste in allow-jit, allow-unsigned-executable-memory and
  disable-library-validation together — gives away two of them to fix a
  problem you may not have. Start with the one that is structurally required
  and add others only when a real failure names them.

  allow-jit is structurally required: the agent is a compiled JavaScript
  runtime, V8 allocates executable memory at startup with mmap(MAP_JIT), and
  the hardened runtime denies that without this. The binary then dies on
  launch with an out-of-executable-memory error — AFTER notarizing cleanly,
  because notarization checks the signature and never runs the program.

  Not here, and worth knowing why:

  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — would be needed if the agent
  itself drove other apps through AppleEvents. It does not: it spawns
  osascript, which is its own process and prompts under its own identity.
  Adding it here would ask the person for a permission nothing uses.

  Screen Recording, Accessibility and Input Monitoring are NOT entitlements.
  They are TCC grants a person flips in System Settings, per application, and
  no signature can grant them. The recorder already checks for them and says
  so rather than recording nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
